#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Rational Exponents Recap 🎓]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Represent roots: $a^(m/n) = root(n, a^m)$ 📏]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("Rational exponents represent roots. The expression a to the power of m over n is equal to the nth root of a to the power of m.")
]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Simplify by converting to radical form and vice versa 🔄]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("To simplify expressions with rational exponents, convert them to radical form and vice versa.")
]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Multiply and divide: $a^(m/n) a^(p/q) = a^((m q+n p)/(n q))$, $a^(m/n) / a^(p/q) = a^((m q-n p)/(n q))$ ✖➗]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("When multiplying expressions with rational exponents, add the exponents. When dividing, subtract the exponents.")
]
#v(20pt)
#only("4-")[- Add and subtract: bases and exponents must be the same ➕➖]
#only("4")[
#voiceover("To add or subtract expressions with rational exponents, the bases and exponents must be the same.")
]
#v(20pt)
#only("5-")[- Solve equations by isolating the term and raising both sides to the reciprocal of the exponent 🧮]
#only("5")[
#voiceover("To solve equations with rational exponents, isolate the term with the exponent and then raise both sides to the reciprocal of the exponent.")
]
]


#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Examples 📝]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[Simplify: $8^(1/3) = 2$ 🎯]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("For example, 8 to the power of one-third equals 2, because the cube root of 8 is 2.")
]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[Multiply: $(27^(1/3))^2 = 9$ ✖]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("27 to the power of one-third, squared, equals 9. This is because the cube root of 27 is 3, and 3 squared is 9.")
]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[Solve: $x^(1/2) = 4 arrow x = 16$ 🧮]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("To solve the equation x to the power of one-half equals 4, we square both sides. This gives us x equals 16.")
]
]

